  'errori.java-nome-file.frase-3': 'ClassName',
  'errori.java-nome-file.frase': `the {nome} class must be in the file {nome2}`,
  'errori.java-nome-file.dove': 'Look at the file name and the `public class` line.',
  'errori.java-nome-file.cosa': 'In Java a `public` class must be in a file with the same name, upper case included: `public class Main` goes in `Main.java`. Rename the file or the class.',
  'errori.java-nome-file.concetto': 'classes and files in Java',
  'errori.java-statico.etichetta': 'non-static used from static',
  'errori.java-statico.frase-2': 'this name',
  'errori.java-statico.frase': `{nome} isn't \`static\`, but you use it from a \`static\` method`,
  'errori.java-statico.dove-2': 'that name',
  'errori.java-statico.dove': `Look at {laRiga} and the declaration of {nome}.`,
  'errori.java-statico.cosa': '`main` is `static`: it belongs to the class, not to an object. From there you can only use `static` methods and variables, or first create an object with `new` and use that. In single-class exercises the method is often just missing `static`.',
  'errori.java-statico.concetto': 'static in Java',
